[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Zhang et al., 2015-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/41dd3b69fbc57953edb83ebd4cebc0186c6b7864)
- venue: BMC Research Notes
- citation count: 35
[method]
The paper describes an S9.6 antibody-based R-loop assay in which S9.6 was purified and used for immunoprecipitation, followed by qPCR to measure R-loop abundance at mammalian IgH switch regions. This method is relevant to the proposed HBD-seq optimization because it represents the antibody-based R-loop detection strategy that the research plan seeks to complement or replace with an antibody-free HBD-based approach; the paper's focus on S9.6 and R-loop detection directly addresses the plan's concern about S9.6 specificity and native R-loop mapping.
[results]
The reported experimental outcome was that S9.6 immunoprecipitation-qPCR identified R-loops at mammalian IgH switch regions, while also showing that cellular RNA complicates R-loop detection. The provided excerpt does not include quantitative values, statistical tests, or comparisons to baseline controls, so no numerical outcome can be reported.
[context]
The assay was performed in cultured cells and targeted IgH switch regions as the genomic loci for R-loop assessment. The workflow included S9.6 purification, S9.6 immunoprecipitation, and qPCR. The supplied excerpt does not specify cell line, antibody concentration, crosslinking or fragmentation conditions, qPCR primer targets, replicate number, or instrument settings.
[end related work 1]

