    "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is that all supplied related work is G4-only and does not directly test the plan's central claim about native co-localized G4s and R-loops. The newer [(Zyner et al., 2022-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d62d57b98bdcab61e2fff24835345c58e9da1894) extends the earlier G4-Pol II observations of [(Shen et al., 2021-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/970cc41034350f60328e48aa6ecab90c6cf895fd) but still uses G4-ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, RNA-seq, and external histone-modification ChIP-seq in pluripotent human embryonic stem cells and 5-day cranial neural crest cell differentiation without R-loop mapping, HBD-seq, S9.6, or Pol II occupancy; therefore its regulatory-region and expression associations cannot be assumed to apply to co-localized G4/R-loop loci identified by HepG4-seq and HBD-seq. [(Shen et al., 2021-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/970cc41034350f60328e48aa6ecab90c6cf895fd) also used G4 ChIP-seq and Pol II ChIP-seq but no R-loop assay, and its antibody-based G4 mapping may shift the equilibrium toward folded G4 states, which is the artifact the plan seeks to avoid with hemin-biotin aniline and EGFP-V5-HBD-based methods. A second contradiction is that [(Shen et al., 2021-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/970cc41034350f60328e48aa6ecab90c6cf895fd) reports that promoter G4 formation does not require active transcription, which complicates the proposed inference that co-localized G4/R-loop peaks near promoters will necessarily correlate with RNA-seq activity or Pol II occupancy; Pol II signal may reflect pausing, chromatin compaction, or G4-associated stalling rather than productive transcription. A third contradiction is that [(Zyner et al., 2022-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d62d57b98bdcab61e2fff24835345c58e9da1894) finds G4 abundance is lost during differentiation and relies on external histone-modification ChIP-seq and an external neural stem cell RNA-seq dataset, GSE8618